keel-transition (0.2.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The pin is 990, not 1001 (tracker#23). Above 1000 apt installed the
    Keel version even when it was older, so every package newer on the
    machine than in the archive was downgraded on the next upgrade. At 990
    the Keel build is still the candidate over any other archive's
    version, and a newer installed version is never replaced.
    tests/turnkey19.bats asks apt itself, from local archives.
  * On a 19.0 appliance --apply replaces what TurnKey's bootstrap left,
    since TurnKey's o=turnkeylinux pin at 999 would beat the Keel pin at
    990: it writes Debian's own debian.sources and security.sources, with
    the suites and components the appliance's Debian stanzas had, then
    renames sources.sources, security.sources.sources,
    turnkey-testing.sources and an /etc/apt/preferences holding only the
    TurnKey pin to <name>.disabled-by-keel, and purges turnkey-keys with
    dpkg -P after keeping the files it owns in /var/lib/keel/transition.
    A debian.sources or security.sources it did not write, other pins in
    /etc/apt/preferences, or a TurnKey stanza in a file it does not know
    is a refusal, exit 5, and then nothing is purged. --rollback renames
    every file back byte for byte, puts the key files back and says to
    reinstall turnkey-keys.
  * keel-transition --version says 0.2.0; it said 0.1.0 since the first
    release.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0000

keel-transition (0.1.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * keel-archive-keyring: ship the rotated public key. Signing subkey
    694DE5E8 was revoked on 2026-09-27 with the offline primary, reason
    compromised, because its secret went through a chat channel on
    2026-09-24; it never signed anything. The new signing subkey is
    03041024F4B2C0C2F42DDDEA04906EAB77513310, Ed25519, expiring 2028-09-26.
    The primary AD0964BE3F09DED469A3B6B2148E951314703180 never left offline
    custody, so the fingerprint this package asserts does not change and a
    machine that already trusts the key keeps trusting it.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 01:39:02 +0000

keel-transition (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release, the migration path of BRIEF section 7, as two binary
    packages built from one source tree.
  * keel-archive-keyring: the project's OpenPGP public key, dearmored at
    build time from keys/keel-archive-keyring.asc and checked against
    fingerprint AD0964BE3F09DED469A3B6B2148E951314703180, with the armored
    copy beside it. No maintainer script and no apt source.
  * keel-transition: /usr/sbin/keel-transition and its library, in three
    phases (survey, --apply, --rollback), each reversible and each
    reported. --apply refuses, with exit 4, an archive whose Release the
    keyring does not verify, unless --force-unsigned is given.
  * No package is ever installed, upgraded or removed by this tool.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
